QB.add(9,[{"n":1,"q":"A 35 y/o male presents with exertional dyspnea and palpitations during sports activities. His family history is significant for a relative who experienced sudden cardiac death at a young age. In managing this patient with suspected hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM), which of the following is a first-line treatment option to relieve symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Enalapril"},{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Candesartan"},{"t":"Surgical myomectomy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Metoprolol which is a b-Adrenergic blocking agent is the first-line treatment for conditions such as hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM), effectively reducing heart rate and improving diastolic filling, thereby alleviating symptoms like exertional dyspnea and palpitations."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medical Management First-line Medications:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"b-Adrenergic Blocking Agents (e.g., Metaprolol): Reduce heart rate and improve diastolic filling. L-type Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., Verapamil): Help decrease contractility and relieve outflow obstruction. Additional Therapy for Persistent Symptoms: Disopyramide: An antiarrhythmic agent with negative inotropic properties to control exertional dyspnea and chest pain."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Surgical and Procedural Surgical Myectomy: Effective in relieving outflow tract Options obstruction by excising part of the septal myocardium."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Alcohol Septal Ablation: Suitable for patients with appropriate coronary anatomy; involves controlled infarction to relieve obstruction. Primarily for patients who prefer to avoid surgery or have comorbidities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HOCM patients are at heightened risk for sudden cardiac death, primarily from ventricular tachyarrhythmias."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physical Activity Guidelines: Traditional prohibitions against vigorous exercise are under reconsideration in ongoing studies."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Indications Recommended for patients with two or more risk factors"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"for SCD. Considered selectively for those with one risk factor. Strongly indicated for patients who have previously survived cardiac arrest due to ventricular fibrillation or sustained ventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"High-Risk Features for Unexplained syncope Primary Prevention Abnormal blood pressure response during exercise"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Significant ventricular hypertrophy (>30mm thickness) Family history of SCD Multiple episodes of non-sustained ventricular tachycardia (NSVT)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Enalapril (Option A) and Candesartan (Option C) which are ACE inhibitors and Angiotensin receptor Blockers respectively are contraindicated in the management of HOCM."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Surgical Myomectomy (Option D) is a surgical intervention reserved for patients with severe symptoms who do not respond to medical management, not a first-line treatment."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 55 y/o woman presents with acute chest pain, difficulty breathing, and signs of heart failure following sudden emotional distress. Her ECG shows ST-segment elevation without reciprocal depression, and echocardiography reveals ballooning of the left ventricular apex. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Myocardial Infarction"},{"t":"Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Aortic Dissection"},{"t":"Yamaguchi Syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation, including acute chest pain, heart failure symptoms, ECG findings of ST-segment elevation without reciprocal depression, and \"takotsubo\" ballooning on echocardiography, points to Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, also known as stress cardiomyopathy. This condition is often triggered by emotional or physical stress, mimicking acute coronary syndrome without coronary artery obstruction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy (Broken Heart syndrome) Yamaguchi Syndrome (Apical Hypertrophic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cardiomyopathy)"},{"k":"img","v":4305},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Definition : A rare form of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy affecting the left ventricle's apex, occasionally the right or both. Epidemiology : More common in Japanese populations (15-25% of HCM cases) compared to the U.S. (~3%). Symptoms : Often asymptomatic; possible symptoms include chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, and syncope. ECG Findings : Left ventricular hypertrophy with deep negative T waves, especially in leads V4-V5. Pathology : Usually sporadic, with occasional autosomal dominant inheritance. Imaging : \"Ace-of-spades\" or \"spade-like\" left ventricle configuration on imaging"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition: Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, or stress configuration on ventriculogram\" data-author=\"A. cardiomyopathy, is a transient condition characterized PayusFarisha SholehN. Mustafa\" data-hash=\"8753\" by left ventricular apical ballooning often triggered by data-license=\"Open access\" data-source=\"https://www.s emotional or physical stress, mimicking acute coronary emanticscholar.org/paper/Yamaguchi-syndrome-%E2%8 syndrome without coronary obstruction. 0%93-A-pseudoacute-coronary-of-the-Payus-Sholeh/6aff Epidemiology : Predominantly affects postmenopausal 765d73025529152e4756f377ead16487bb54/figure/3\" women, triggered by emotional or physical stress. data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com Clinical Presentation : Chest pain, signs of acute heart /notes/BknugIRWXDkiz5PAHEME1744301744.png\" /> failure, resembling acute coronary syndrome. {{caption_text}} ECG : ST-segment elevation, absent reciprocal depression, T-wave inversion, mild QT prolongation. Biochemistry : Elevated catecholamines, BNP, and modest troponin rise. Imaging : Echocardiography : Classic \"takotsubo\" ballooning of the left ventricular apex, with basal segments hyperdynamic and apex hypokinetic. Can show left ventricular outflow tract obstruction or mitral regurgitation. MRI : Apical ballooning with high T2 intensity (oedema), typically sparing the basal segments. No late enhancement, differentiating it from anterior STEMI. Left Ventriculography : Demonstrates transient dysfunction and ballooning of mid and apical segments of the left ventricle. Prognosis : Excellent recovery with supportive treatment, typically resolves in weeks to months."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction (Option A) typically presents with ST-segment elevation and reciprocal depression, which is absent here."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic dissection (Option C) would present with severe chest pain radiating to the back and may not cause ST-segment elevation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Yamaguchi syndrome (Option D) (apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy) involves hypertrophy and apical thickening but does not present with acute chest pain or ST-segment elevation."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 55-year-old patient presents with sharp chest pain, fever, and pleuritis 3 weeks after undergoing coronary artery bypass surgery. Which of the following is the most likely underlying mechanism?","o":[{"t":"Bacterial pericarditis"},{"t":"Hypersensitivity reaction to myocardial antigens"},{"t":"Direct trauma from the procedure"},{"t":"Viral infection"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient’s presentation is consistent with postcardiac injury syndrome, often resulting from an autoimmune response to antigens from the injured myocardium following surgery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Post-cardiac injury syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition : Postcardiac Injury Syndrome is acute pericarditis triggered by prior injury to the heart, leading to blood in the pericardial cavity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes : Can follow cardiac surgery, trauma, catheterization, or occasionally a myocardial infarction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms : Sharp chest pain (mimicking viral/idiopathic pericarditis), fever, pleuritis, and pneumonitis, typically appearing 1–4 weeks after injury. Recurrences can happen over several years."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology : Likely due to an autoimmune reaction against myocardial/pericardial antigens."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment : Usually self-limiting; managed with aspirin or NSAIDs. Severe or recurring cases may require colchicine or glucocorticoids."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bacterial pericarditis (Option A) : Unlikely, as it typically presents sooner after surgery and with signs of sepsis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Direct trauma from the procedure (Option C) : Less likely, as trauma-related pain would generally occur immediately post-surgery, not weeks later."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Viral infection (Option D) : Unlikely, as viral pericarditis is not typically related to recent cardiac surgery or triggered by myocardial antigens."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A post-mortem study reveals fibrinous deposits between the pericardial leaflets with a “bread and butter” appearance. Which of the following is most consistent with this form of pericarditis?","o":[{"t":"Hemorrhagic pericarditis"},{"t":"Purulent pericarditis"},{"t":"Fibrinous pericarditis"},{"t":"Caseous pericarditis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The “bread and butter” appearance is characteristic of fibrinous pericarditis, marked by fibrin and leukocytic exudate on the pericardium."},{"k":"img","v":4312},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bread and butter appearance seen in fibrinous pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types of Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fibrinous pericarditis Often called \"bread and butter\" pericarditis due to fibrin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"deposits creating a rough appearance on the pericardium. Caused by conditions like myocardial infarction, infections, trauma, or systemic diseases. Associated with a granular, opaque pericardial surface from fibrin and leukocytic exudate."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serous pericarditis Characterized by clear fluid accumulation, often seen in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"viral infections or autoimmune diseases. Less intense inflammation than fibrinous type."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Purulent pericarditis Caused by bacterial infections, leading to a pus-filled (Option B ruled out) pericardial sac."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Requires urgent treatment to prevent severe complications."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Haemorrhagic Involves blood within the pericardium, often due to pericarditis trauma, malignancy, or uremia. (Option A ruled out) Common in cases with malignancies like lung or breast"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cancer."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Caseous pericarditis Rare and typically associated with tuberculosis. (Option D ruled out) Has a cheese-like appearance due to necrotic tissue in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"the pericardium."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 50 y/o female with a history of heart failure presents with worsening fatigue, shortness of breath, and leg swelling. The Chest X-ray and Cardiac MRI of the patient are given below. Which of the following statements regarding the treatment of her condition is FALSE?","o":[{"t":"Oral diuretics should be administered to maintain a euvolemic state."},{"t":"ACE inhibitors are recommended for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction."},{"t":"Beta-blockers should not be used in patients with symptomatic heart failure."},{"t":"Isosorbide dinitrate plus hydralazine combination therapy may improve survival."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms of worsening fatigue, shortness of breath, and leg swelling, combined with her history of heart failure, indicate a likely exacerbation of her condition due to potential ventricular dysfunction or volume overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These findings, alongside imaging studies like chest X-rays showing cardiomegaly and cardiac MRI also showing dilated chambers with thin ventricular walls, support a diagnosis of dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) or worsening heart failure related to it."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-blockers are actually recommended for patients with symptomatic heart failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray: May show cardiomegaly, pulmonary Identify and Treat Reversible Causes: Address any effusions, and venous congestion. underlying and reversible conditions contributing to Electrocardiogram: Shows nonspecific ST segment and DCM. T wave abnormalities; can reveal atrial fibrillation. Acute Congestive Heart Failure: Use intravenous loop Echocardiography: Can identify mural thrombus and diuretics to manage hypervolemia. (Option A ruled out) differentiate from other cardiomyopathies. Chronic Management: Administer oral diuretics to Cardiac MRI . maintain a euvolemic state. Cardiac Catheterization with Coronary Angiography: Medical management: Assesses coronary anatomy and rules out ischemic ACE Inhibitors/ARBs (Option B ruled out) disease. Aldosterone Receptor Antagonists Heart Biopsy: Rarely needed; evaluates for storage Beta-Blockers diseases or infiltrative causes. Combination Therapy: Isosorbide dinitrate plus Genetic Testing: Considered in familial cases to identify hydralazine. (Option D ruled out) hereditary factors. Anticoagulation: Use in patients with artificial valves,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"atrial fibrillation, or known mural thrombus to reduce stroke risk. Advanced Therapies: Consider cardiac transplantation or left ventricular assist devices (LVAD) for refractory cases. ICD and CRT: Evaluate for implanted cardioverter defibrillators and cardiac resynchronization therapy for appropriate patients. Heart Transplant: An option for severe cases, with indications including refractory cardiogenic shock and high inotrope dependency."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest X-Ray Cardiac MRI"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"cardiomyopathy chest x ray\" data-author=\"James cardiomyopathy cardiac MRI\" data-author=\"Praveen Heilman, MD\" data-hash=\"8738\" data-license=\"CC Jha\" data-hash=\"8728\" data-license=\"\" BY-SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dil data-source=\"https://radiopaedia.org/cases/dilated-cardi ated_cardiomyopathy#/media/File:DifDilatedCardiomyoM omyopathy-4\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"385\" agCXR.png\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"400\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/qSo3iyFqbS2y0"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5gb84se91744301178.png\" width=\"355\" /> {{caption_text}} Gross Specimen Cardiac Biopsy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"cardiomyopathy gross specimen\" data-author=\"CDC/Dr. cardiac biopsy vs dilated cardiomyopathy biopsy\" Edwin P. Ewing, Jr.\" data-hash=\"8733\" data-author=\"Michael Chute, Preetinder Aujla, Sayantan data-license=\"Open access\" data-source=\"https://en.wiki Jana and Zamaneh Kassiri\" data-hash=\"8737\" pedia.org/wiki/Cardiomyopathy#/media/File:Idiopathic_ca data-license=\"CC BY 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons. rdiomyopathy,_gross_pathology_20G0018_lores.jpg\" wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Histopathology_of_interstitial_fibr data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"281\" osis_in_dilated_cardiomyopathy.jpg\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"cUivnjaBc1744301001.png\" width=\"407\" /> src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/YpKUkuILzaUx {{caption_text}} UwrlUKlq1744301098.png\" width=\"755\" />"}],"qi":[4247,4253]},{"n":6,"q":"A 6-year-old child presents with increasing fatigue, shortness of breath during physical activity, and swelling in the legs. His mother reports that he has difficulty lying flat at night due to breathlessness. The child appears malnourished with signs of muscle wasting. On examination, there are crackles in the lung fields and elevated jugular venous pressure. An ECG reveals left ventricular enlargement. Which of the following findings is most consistent with the clinical features observed in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Ejection fraction usually >55%"},{"t":"Left ventricular diastolic dimension ‡60 mm if chronic"},{"t":"Right ventricular dysfunction with no valvular regurgitation"},{"t":"Point of maximum impulse displaced medially"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical features of fatigue, respiratory distress, oedema, and left ventricular enlargement in a malnourished child suggest wet beriberi, with chronic left ventricular dilation (‡60 mm) being a consistent finding due to thiamine deficiency impairing myocardial function., characterized by impaired systolic function and ventricular dilation. These findings align with the typical presentation of heart failure due to DCM."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In chronic dilated cardiomyopathy, the left ventricular diastolic dimension can be significantly enlarged, typically more than 60 mm, indicating ventricular dilation. This aligns with the patient's symptoms and findings."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most cases present between ages 20 and 60, but it can occur in children and the elderly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Patients may have a long asymptomatic period; symptoms arise from left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Common First Symptom: Exertional intolerance. Classic Symptoms: Include paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea, leg swelling, and shortness of breath. Severe Cases: Can lead to thromboembolic complications, conduction disturbances, arrhythmias, or sudden cardiac death. Congestive Symptoms: Left-sided before right-sided, except for right prominence in young adults. Nonspecific Symptoms: Fatigue, malaise, and weakness may also be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients may have a long asymptomatic period; symptoms arise from left ventricular systolic dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common First Symptom: Exertional intolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic Symptoms: Include paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea, leg swelling, and shortness of breath."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe Cases: Can lead to thromboembolic complications, conduction disturbances, arrhythmias, or sudden cardiac death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congestive Symptoms: Left-sided before right-sided, except for right prominence in young adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonspecific Symptoms: Fatigue, malaise, and weakness may also be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physical Exam Findings: Crackles in lung fields Elevated jugular venous pressures Peripheral oedema S3 gallop"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crackles in lung fields"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated jugular venous pressures"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral oedema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S3 gallop"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Point of Maximum Impulse (PMI) displaced laterally. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Murmurs: Tricuspid or mitral regurgitation murmurs may be heard due to ventricular enlargement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May reveal jugular venous distension, A-wave, large V waves, and positive hepatojugular reflux."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arrhythmias: Ventricular tachyarrhythmia; conduction block in Chagas' disease and some genetic etiologies; atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ejection Fraction: Usually <30% when symptoms are severe (normal >55%). (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left Ventricular Diastolic Dimension: Typically ‡60 mm if chronic (normal <55 mm)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left Ventricular Wall Thickness: Normal or decreased."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial Size: Increased, with left atrium before right atrium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Valvular Regurgitation: Related to annular and ventricular dilation; mitral regurgitation appears earlier during decompensation; tricuspid regurgitation occurs with right ventricular dysfunction. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients may have a long asymptomatic period; symptoms arise from left ventricular systolic dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common First Symptom: Exertional intolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic Symptoms: Include paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea, leg swelling, and shortness of breath."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe Cases: Can lead to thromboembolic complications, conduction disturbances, arrhythmias, or sudden cardiac death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congestive Symptoms: Left-sided before right-sided, except for right prominence in young adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonspecific Symptoms: Fatigue, malaise, and weakness may also be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crackles in lung fields"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated jugular venous pressures"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral oedema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S3 gallop"},{"k":"img","v":4318}],"qi":[4259]},{"n":7,"q":"A 55 y/o male presents to the emergency department with fatigue, shortness of breath, and swollen legs. Upon examination, his heart on imaging revealed the following, and his left ventricular ejection fraction is markedly reduced. Which of the following is NOT a recognised cause of this patient’s diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Sarcoidosis"},{"t":"Chagas disease"},{"t":"Scleroderma"},{"t":"Heavy metal exposure to lead"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient presents with symptoms of fatigue, shortness of breath, and swollen legs, alongside imaging showing an enlarged heart and a markedly reduced left ventricular ejection fraction, indicative of dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma is primarily a connective tissue disease and is not directly recognized as a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy but is linked to Restrictive cardiomyopathy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is characterised by an enlarged left ventricle and reduced systolic function, as evidenced by a decreased left ventricular ejection fraction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It predominantly features systolic failure, while diastolic dysfunction is less prominent."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DCM can result from various etiologies, leading to common pathways of secondary response and disease progression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Dilated Cardiomyopathy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infective Noninfective Viral ( coxsackievirus , adenovirus, HIV, hepatitis C) Granulomatous inflammatory disease Parasitic ( Chagas’ disease , trypanosomiasis, Sarcoidosis (Option A ruled out) toxoplasmosis) (Option B ruled out) Giant cell myocarditis Bacterial ( diphtheria ) Eosinophilic myocarditis Spirochetal ( Lyme disease) Polymyositis, dermatomyositis Rickettsial (Q fever) Collagen vascular disease Fungal (with systemic infection) Checkpoint inhibitor chemotherapy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Transplant rejection"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Toxic Metabolic Alcohol Nutritional deficiencies ( thiamine, selenium , carnitine) Catecholamines (amphetamines, cocaine) Electrolyte deficiencies (calcium, phosphate, Chemotherapeutic agents (anthracyclines, magnesium) trastuzumab ) Endocrinopathy (thyroid disease, pheochromocytoma, Interferon diabetes, obesity, hemochromatosis) Other therapeutic agents ( hydroxychloroquine , Skeletal and cardiac myopathy chloroquine) Dystrophin-related dystrophy (Duchenne’s, Becker’s) Drugs of misuse (emetine, anabolic steroids) Mitochondrial myopathies (e.g., Kearns-Sayre syndrome ) Heavy metals ( lead, mercury ) (Option D ruled out) Occupational exposure (hydrocarbons, arsenicals)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Overlap with Nondilated Cardiomyopathy Miscellaneous Minimally dilated cardiomyopathy Arrhythmogenic ventricular cardiomyopathy Hemochromatosis Peripartum cardiomyopathy Amyloidosis Left ventricular noncompaction Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (“burned-out”) Tachycardia-related cardiomyopathy Idiopathic Supraventricular arrhythmias with uncontrolled rate"}],"qi":[4265]},{"n":8,"q":"A 55-year-old male is being evaluated for restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM). Which of the following statements regarding the investigations and treatment options for RCM is FALSE?","o":[{"t":"An echocardiogram is the primary test for diagnosing RCM."},{"t":"Low-to-normal QRS voltage on ECG can suggest amyloidosis."},{"t":"Therapeutic phlebotomy is the primary treatment for sarcoidosis."},{"t":"Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing cardiac amyloidosis."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations Treatment"},{"k":"img","v":4325},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Treat Underlying Causes: Focus on alleviating symptoms and managing heart failure. Diuretics: Mainstay for reducing volume overload; must be monitored to avoid excessive diuresis. Beta-blockers/Calcium Channel Blockers: May be used to increase filling time and manage dysrhythmias. Angiotensin-Receptor Blockers: Considered if systolic"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG: Low-to-normal QRS voltage may suggest"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"heart failure develops."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"amyloidosis, but the absence of low voltage does not"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"For Sarcoidosis: Antiarrhythmics and"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"rule it out. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"immunosuppressive agents like corticosteroids may"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Echocardiogram:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"be used."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is the primary test for RCM and helps differentiate it"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"For Hemochromatosis: Therapeutic phlebotomy is the"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"from conditions like constrictive pericarditis. (Option A"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"treatment of choice. (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Advanced Treatments: Cardiac transplant or left"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thickened walls of both ventricles without major"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ventricular assist devices may be options for some"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"chamber dilation."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The atria are markedly dilated, indicating chronically elevated ventricular filling pressures. There is a characteristic hyperrefractile “glittering” appearance of the myocardium, typical of amyloid infiltration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Granular sparkling heart Cardiac Magnetic Resonance (CMR): Useful for diagnosing RCM and suggests amyloidosis through gadolinium enhancement. Endomyocardial Biopsy (EMB): Gold standard for diagnosing cardiac amyloidosis; fat pad aspiration is also beneficial. (Option D ruled out) Nuclear Imaging: Can show affinity for amyloid and aid in diagnosis. Biomarkers: Troponin T, BNP, and pro-BNP are useful for diagnosis and prognosis. Mutation Analysis: Used to diagnose hereditary hemochromatosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Echocardiography showing enlarged atria and thickened ventricle walls"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 60 y/o male with a history of amyloidosis presents with shortness of breath, fatigue, and leg swelling. He has jugular venous distension and ascites. An echocardiogram shows normal systolic function but diastolic dysfunction. The chest X-ray of the patient is given below. Which of the following statements is true?","o":[{"t":"The atria are severely shrunken."},{"t":"Jugular venous pressure often shows rapid Y descents."},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation is rare in these patients."},{"t":"A third heart sound is more common than a fourth heart sound in sinus rhythm."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis in this case is Restrictive Cardiomyopathy (RCM) , often associated with conditions like amyloidosis. The patient's symptoms—shortness of breath, fatigue, leg swelling, jugular venous distension, and ascites—are indicative of heart failure due to restricted ventricularfilling. Chest X-ray shows right heart margin enlargement, likely due to left atrial dilation causingan overlap sign. The echocardiogram findings further support this diagnosis, showing normalsystolic function with evidence of diastolic dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with RCM, particularly those with amyloidosis, you can also observe specific signs like rapid Y descents in jugular venous pressure, which is consistent with the heart's impaired ability to fill."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prominent y descent : Due to elevated atrial pressures, there is rapid early ventricular filling in diastole, creating a pronounced y descent on atrial pressure curves."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blunted x descent : Poor atrial relaxation and limited annular descent toward the apex result in a reduced x descent, as the atrium does not effectively empty or relax."},{"k":"img","v":4328},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"RA hemodynamic pressure tracings in restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM). A prominent “y” descent is present, but the “x” descent is blunted."},{"k":"img","v":4331},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Restrictive Cardiomyopathy (RCM)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically shows a normal or near-normal systolic function with evidence of diastolic dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart Failure Symptoms: May present with jugular venous distension, ascites, lower extremity oedema, and less commonly, pulmonary oedema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common first symptoms include exertional intolerance and fluid retention, diagnosed arrhythmias, such as atrial fibrillation and SCD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extracardiac Manifestations causing RCM:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Amyloidosis Carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis Bilateral Hair infiltrates"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis Bronze skin, cirrhosis, Arthralgias"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Endocrinopathies Diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac Impulse: Less displaced than in dilated cardiomyopathy and less dynamic than in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ejection fraction is usually >40-50%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The left ventricular diastolic dimension is typically <60 mm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular wall thickness is normal or increased."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial size may be massively increased and equal in both atria. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Valvular regurgitation is common, especially mitral and tricuspid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right-sided symptoms often predominate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation is very common (Option C ruled out) ; ventricular arrhythmias are rare."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A fourth heart sound is more common than a third heart sound in sinus rhythm, with atrial fibrillation being frequent. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Jugular Venous Pressure: Often shows rapid Y descents and may increase during inspiration ( positive Kussmaul’s sign )."}],"qi":[4271]},{"n":10,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with worsening fatigue, shortness of breath, and ankle swelling. His medical history includes joint pain, elevated liver enzymes, hyperpigmentation, and diabetes. He has recently been diagnosed with a metabolic disorder. An echocardiogram reveals elevated filling pressures and bilateral atrial enlargement. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"Increased contractility due to myocardial hypertrophy"},{"t":"Impaired diastolic filling due to stiffened ventricular walls"},{"t":"Dilated ventricular chambers leading to heart failure"},{"t":"Obstruction of blood flow through the left ventricle"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms and echocardiographic findings suggest diastolic dysfunction, likely due to a condition such as hemochromatosis that leads to ventricular wall stiffness. The stiffened ventricular walls impede proper filling during diastole, leading to increased end-diastolic pressures and symptoms of heart failure, such as fatigue and peripheral oedema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characterized by abnormal diastolic function, often with mildly reduced contractility and ejection fraction (usually 30–50%)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infiltrative diseases : Amyloidosis (Most common cause) Sarcoidosis Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amyloidosis (Most common cause)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fibrotic conditions : Radiation therapy Scleroderma Endomyocardial fibrosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endomyocardial fibrosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Storage diseases : Glycogen storage diseases (types II, III) Fabry's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glycogen storage diseases (types II, III)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fabry's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amyloidosis (Most common cause)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Scleroderma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endomyocardial fibrosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glycogen storage diseases (types II, III)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fabry's disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Abnormal Diastolic The primary abnormality in restrictive cardiomyopathy is Function impaired diastolic filling due to the stiffened ventricular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"walls. Elevated end-diastolic pressures in both ventricles without significant chamber dilation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bilateral Atrial The non-compliant ventricles lead to increased Enlargement pressures, resulting in dilation of both atria."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Right-Sided Heart Failure The stiff ventricles result in increased filling pressures,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"particularly affecting the right side, causing peripheral oedema, ascites, and jugular venous distention."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Amyloid Infiltration (in Amyloid fibrils (pink hyaline deposits) accumulate amyloidosis) between myocytes, leading to myocardial stiffening and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"restrictive physiology. amyloidosis in congo red stain\" data-author=\"Novikov, Aleksey & Holzer, Horatio & DeSimone, Robert & Abu-Zeinah, Ghaith & Pisapia, David & Mark, Tomer & Pastore, Raymond.\" data-hash=\"8672\" data-license=\"CC0\" data-source=\"https://www.researchg ate.net/figure/Cardiac-amyloidosis-A-Congo-red-stain-of- left-ventricle-showing-focal-positive_fig1_284096165\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/dJpPe8RwpqgEgabbGhLT1744298841.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cardiac amyloidosis (Congo red stain)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fibrosis In conditions like radiation-induced cardiomyopathy or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"endomyocardial fibrosis, stiffening of the myocardium occurs due to scarring, further impairing ventricular filling."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased contractility due to myocardial hypertrophy (Option A) is incorrect because restrictive cardiomyopathy typically exhibits preserved or mildly reduced contractility, not increased contractility."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dilated ventricular chambers leading to heart failure (Option C) do not apply, as dilated ventricular chambers are not characteristic of restrictive cardiomyopathy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Obstruction of blood flow through the left ventricle (Option D) is also incorrect, as obstruction of blood flow is more commonly associated with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy rather than restrictive cardiomyopathy."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 30-year-old female marathon runner collapses and is found unresponsive during a race. Despite attempts at resuscitation, she is pronounced dead at the scene. Her family reports a history of sudden cardiac death in her brother at a young age, and they mention that she had previously experienced episodes of dizziness and palpitations during training. Which of the following conditions is most likely to contribute to sudden cardiac death in this young athlete?","o":[{"t":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)"},{"t":"Commotio cordis"},{"t":"Valvular heart disease"},{"t":"Atherosclerotic coronary artery disease"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes, especially in those with a family history of sudden death, like this patient. The symptoms of dizziness and palpitations during training strongly suggest HCM."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sudden Cardiac Death (SCD) is defined as unexpected death occurring within an hour of symptom onset due to cardiac causes, including unwitnessed deaths in individuals who seemed healthy within the last 24 hours."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risk Factors Structural Heart Disease: The presence of structural"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"heart issues or inherited arrhythmia syndromes significantly increases SCD risk. Major Risk Factors: Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) Heart Failure Diabetes Hypertension Smoking Family History of SCD"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Precipitating SCD and Sudden Cardiac Arrest (SCA) frequently occur Factors at specific times (e.g., mornings, winter months) and in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"public places. Vigorous exertion, especially in men, can trigger SCD; however, regular exercise may reduce this risk."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Underlying Heart The most common cause of SCD is Ventricular fibrillation Disease Many SCD cases are linked to coronary artery disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(CAD), but non-ischemic cardiomyopathies and other heart conditions also contribute. Common mechanisms leading to SCD include: Acute Myocardial Infarction Bradyarrhythmias Ventricular scars leading to arrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Commotio cordis is a specific cause of SCD, often resulting from an impact to the left chest by a hard object, such as a baseball, during sports. This can induce ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest in an otherwise structurally normal heart. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Younger Athletes: The leading causes of SCD are inherited cardiac conditions, particularly Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Older Athletes: Risks are primarily associated with atherosclerotic coronary artery disease (CAD)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy (ARVC):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARVC is recognised as a cause of ventricular tachycardia and SCD in young athletes, characterized by: Progressive fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricle Familial transmission Symptomatic arrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricle"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Familial transmission"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic arrhythmias"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Young athletes with ARVC may exhibit symptoms such as palpitations, dizziness, and unexplained syncope."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricle"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Familial transmission"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic arrhythmias"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Valvular heart disease (Option C) and Atherosclerotic coronary artery disease (Option D) are less likely to be the cause in a young, fit athlete."}]},{"n":12,"q":"Which of the following medications is not contraindicated in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","o":[{"t":"Amlodipine"},{"t":"Verapamil"},{"t":"Furosemide"},{"t":"Digoxin"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Verapamil, a calcium channel blocker, is not contraindicated and can be beneficial in managing symptoms in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. It helps decrease contractility and relieve outflow obstruction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In HOCM , the left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction worsens when the heart is under-filled (low preload) or when the heart has to contract more forcefully."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low preload results in reduced end-diastolic volume, exacerbating outflow obstruction due to a narrower channel."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased contractility raises blood flow across the outflow tract, causing greater anterior displacement of the mitral leaflet, further obstructing the LVOT."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is why certain drugs are contraindicated in HOCM, as they can exacerbate the obstruction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drugs contraindicated in Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nitroglycerin: It causes vasodilation , which reduces preload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide: It causes diuresis, which leads to a reduction in blood volume hence a decrease in preload. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors : ACE inhibitors reduce afterload (the resistance the heart must pump against) and can also reduce preload by causing vasodilation and decreasing blood volume."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angiotensin receptor blockers: ARBs work similarly to ACE inhibitors by reducing afterload and preload ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digoxin : It works by increasing the contractility of the heart (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amlodipine : It is a calcium channel blocker causing peripheral vasodilation, hence causing decreased preload. (Option A ruled out)"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 45 y/o male presents with sharp, pleuritic chest pain worsened by lying down and relieved by sitting up. He had a recent viral illness. A friction rub is heard on auscultation, and ECG shows the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Myocardial infarction"},{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Acute pericarditis"},{"t":"Aortic dissection"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation and ECG findings of diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR-segment depression point to acute pericarditis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology Clinical Features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infectious causes Noninfectious causes Acute Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Viruses : Coxsackieviru Renal failure Chest pain s, echovirus, herpes Neoplasms Sharp, pleuritic pain, worsened by viruses. Trauma lying down, relieved by sitting up . Bacteria : Streptococcu Aortic dissection Radiates to trapezius, may mimic s, Staphylococcus, Myocardial infarction, myocardial ischemia. tuberculosis. post-irradiation, metabolic disorders Pericardial friction rub Fungal infections : (myxedema, cholesterol pericarditis). Rasping and scratching sounds are Histoplasmosis. Drug-induced : Medications like heard in the majority of patients. It is Other infections : procainamide, and hydralazine. best heard during end-expiration Syphilis, protozoal, and Post-cardiac injury syndrome: with the patient leaning forward. parasitic infections. Autoimmune reaction after surgery, Pericardial effusion :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"trauma. It can lead to cardiac tamponade if Autoimmune diseases : Lupus, rapid. Faint heart sounds, and a rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma. large cardiac silhouette on X-ray. Idiopathic. Ewart’s sign : Dullness and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"egophony below the left scapula due to compression of the base of the lungs ECG changes : Diffuse concave upward ST-segment elevation, PR-segment depression . Evolves through stages: ST elevation fi return to baseline fi T-wave inversion fi normalisation. PR segment depression in MI patients is seen due to concomitant atrial injury."},{"k":"img","v":4340},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG of Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Purple arrow: ST elevation Red arrow: PR segment depression"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction (Option A): Typically causes chest pain that is described as a heavy, crushing discomfort rather than the sharp, pleuritic pain seen in this case. While the pain can radiate to the left arm or jaw, myocardial infarction is more likely to present with localised ST-segment elevation but not PR-segment depression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary embolism (Option B): This condition often presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain, shortness of breath, and sometimes hemoptysis. However, the ECG findings in pulmonary embolism can include a right ventricular strain pattern or the classic S1Q3T3 sign, rather than the widespread ST elevation and PR-segment depression."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic dissection (Option D): This is Characterized by a tearing or ripping chest pain that typically radiates to the back or abdomen. While it can be life-threatening and requires urgent treatment, the pain in aortic dissection is usually not pleuritic or positional. ECG findings are non-specific in Aortic Dissection."}],"qi":[4275]},{"n":14,"q":"A 30 y/o male presents with exertional chest pain and occasional fainting spells. His family history includes a relative who experienced sudden cardiac death. An echocardiogram reveals asymmetric septal hypertrophy. Which of the following is not a characteristic investigatory finding in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Brockenbrough-Braunwald-Morrow sign"},{"t":"Deep, narrow Q waves in the lateral leads"},{"t":"Left ventricle free wall thickness >10 mm"},{"t":"Increased precordial voltages"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical scenario suggests hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM) due to the athlete's exertional syncope and family history of sudden cardiac death, alongside echocardiographic findings of asymmetric septal hypertrophy, which are characteristic of HOCM."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, left ventricle free wall thickness >10 mm is incorrect as HOCM is characterized by left ventricle thickness >15 mm (typically >30 mm in severe cases)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations for HOCM"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Trans-Thoracic Echocardiography (T.T.E)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricle free wall thickness >15 mm (> 30 mm, higher risk of sudden cardiac death)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Systolic anterior movement of the mitral valve"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymmetrical septal hypertrophy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG features of HOCM"},{"k":"img","v":4344},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular hypertrophy ( Sokolow-Lyon criteria : S wave depth in V1 + tallest R wave height in V5 or V6 > 35 mm) with increased precordial voltages and non-specific ST segment and T-wave abnormalities (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep, narrow (“dagger-like”) Q waves in lateral (I, aVL, V5-6) +/- inferior (II, III, aVF) leads ( Option B ruled out) due to septal hypertrophy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"lateral and inferior leads in HOCM\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8660\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"437\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"width=\"437\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac catheterization:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In HOCM, left ventricular (LV) outflow obstruction due to thickened septal muscle creates a systolic pressure gradient between the LV and the aortic valve, illustrated by the Brockenbrough–Braunwald–Morrow sign , which occurs when a premature ventricular contraction leads to a decrease in aortic pressure and an increase in LV pressure during the subsequent contraction. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Square root sign : Seen in constrictive pericarditis and restrictive cardiomyopathy. Early diastolic filling : At the beginning of diastole, the ventricles fill quickly because there's no obstruction initially. This is the \"dip\" in the pattern. Late diastolic filling : However, as the ventricles continue to fill, they reach a limit quickly because the stiff pericardium restricts any further expansion. This leads to an abrupt stop in filling, causing the pressure to level off, which creates the \"plateau\" or flat line in the pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early diastolic filling : At the beginning of diastole, the ventricles fill quickly because there's no obstruction initially. This is the \"dip\" in the pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Late diastolic filling : However, as the ventricles continue to fill, they reach a limit quickly because the stiff pericardium restricts any further expansion. This leads to an abrupt stop in filling, causing the pressure to level off, which creates the \"plateau\" or flat line in the pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early diastolic filling : At the beginning of diastole, the ventricles fill quickly because there's no obstruction initially. This is the \"dip\" in the pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Late diastolic filling : However, as the ventricles continue to fill, they reach a limit quickly because the stiff pericardium restricts any further expansion. This leads to an abrupt stop in filling, causing the pressure to level off, which creates the \"plateau\" or flat line in the pattern."},{"k":"img","v":4347}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 15-year-old male presents to the ED after experiencing syncope while playing basketball. He has a palpable double impulse at the apex, and an ECG shows signs of left ventricular hypertrophy. An echocardiogram reveals asymmetric septal thickening with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction. Which of the following examination findings would NOT be expected in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Palpable double impulse at the apex"},{"t":"Late-peaking ejection systolic murmur best heard at the left sternal border"},{"t":"Pulsus bisferiens on peripheral pulse examination"},{"t":"Prominent y descent in jugular venous pressure (JVP)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms of syncope during exertion, left ventricular hypertrophy, and asymmetric septal thickening with outflow tract obstruction strongly suggest hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) , specifically hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM) . Prominent y descent in jugular venous pressure (JVP) is more typical of constrictive pericarditis rather than HOCM."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Examination Findings :"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Finding Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Palpable Left Ventricular An enlarged left ventricle can be felt during physical Hypertrophy examination, indicating hypertrophy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Finding Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Double Impulse at the A palpable fourth heart sound suggests forceful atrial Apex contraction against a non-compliant ventricle. (Option A"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mid-Systolic Murmur at Turbulent blood flow through the obstructed left the Base ventricular outflow tract produces a mid-systolic murmur."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pansystolic Murmur at the Indicates mitral regurgitation caused by abnormal Apex systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Jerky Arterial Pulse Characteristic of HOCM, contrasts with the slow-rising"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pulse seen in aortic stenosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pulsus bisferiens (Option A double pulse noticed during systole in the peripheral C ruled out) pulse is called pulsus bisferiens. It is associated with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"severe aortic disease accompanied by aortic regurgitation and HOCM."},{"k":"img","v":4350},{"k":"img","v":4353},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"JVP in HCOM:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prominent \"a\" wave due to increased atrial pressure as the right atrium contracts against a stiff ventricle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May have elevated JVP"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Auscultatory Findings in Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (HOCM):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Auscultatory Feature Description Clinical Relevance"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ejection Systolic Murmur Late-onset crescendo-decrescend Caused by turbulent blood flow (Option B ruled out) o murmur. through the obstructed LVOT; best"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"heard at the left sternal border or apex."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dynamic Changes with Increased Intensity: Occurs with Indicates worsening obstruction. Maneuvers decreased preload (e.g., Valsalva"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"manoeuvre, amyl nitrite)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased Intensity: Occurs Suggests improvement in outflow with increased afterload (e.g., tract obstruction. handgrip, inspiration)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-Ejection Systolic Clicks Clicks due to asymmetrical Indicates dynamic changes in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hypertrophy affecting the mitral mitral apparatus, though less chordae tendinae. common."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abnormal Second Heart May show delayed aortic closure Correlates with the severity of left Sound (S2) with patterns of reverse splitting. ventricular outflow obstruction;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"reversed or partially reversed splitting indicates severe obstruction."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 30-year-old competitive athlete presents with recurrent syncope and exertional chest pain. He has a jerky, forceful pulse. ECG reveals left ventricular hypertrophy with deep Q waves in the inferolateral leads, and troponin T is mildly elevated. Echocardiography shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, and a mid-systolic murmur at the heart's base. What is the most likely mechanism for his symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Diastolic heart failure due to a dilated left ventricle"},{"t":"Mitral valve prolapse"},{"t":"Left ventricular outflow tract obstruction"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The young athlete’s symptoms, combined with asymmetric septal hypertrophy and ECG findings, strongly suggest hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The mid-systolic murmur indicates obstruction to blood flow from the left ventricle during systole, which aligns with the presentation of HOCM ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left ventricular outflow tract obstruction is the hallmark of HOCM caused by asymmetric septal hypertrophy leading to dynamic obstruction during systole, which explains his effort-related symptoms and murmur."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (HOCM)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autosomal dominant disorder (a genetic disorder)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mutations : Beta-myosin heavy-chain : Leads to significant ventricular hypertrophy. Troponin : Minimal hypertrophy but associated with myocardial fibre disarray and sudden death. Myosin-binding protein C : Often presents later in life; linked to hypertension and arrhythmias."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-myosin heavy-chain : Leads to significant ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Troponin : Minimal hypertrophy but associated with myocardial fibre disarray and sudden death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myosin-binding protein C : Often presents later in life; linked to hypertension and arrhythmias."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key Characteristics : Inappropriate, extensive left ventricular hypertrophy. Misaligned myocardial fibres (helter skelter disarray) and myocardial fibrosis. A rigid left ventricle impedes diastolic filling, leading to heart failure. Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction (LVOTO) due to septal hypertrophy. Mitral regurgitation due to systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inappropriate, extensive left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misaligned myocardial fibres (helter skelter disarray) and myocardial fibrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A rigid left ventricle impedes diastolic filling, leading to heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction (LVOTO) due to septal hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral regurgitation due to systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-myosin heavy-chain : Leads to significant ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Troponin : Minimal hypertrophy but associated with myocardial fibre disarray and sudden death."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myosin-binding protein C : Often presents later in life; linked to hypertension and arrhythmias."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inappropriate, extensive left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misaligned myocardial fibres (helter skelter disarray) and myocardial fibrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A rigid left ventricle impedes diastolic filling, leading to heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction (LVOTO) due to septal hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral regurgitation due to systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve."},{"k":"img","v":4357},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effort-Related Symptoms : Angina : Due to increased myocardial oxygen demand during exertion. Dyspnoea : Resulting from impaired diastolic filling and inefficient LV function. Syncope : Caused by reduced cardiac output during exertion. Sudden Death : The most common cause of sudden death in HOCM is ventricular fibrillation.High risk during intense physical activity, especially in young athletes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angina : Due to increased myocardial oxygen demand during exertion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnoea : Resulting from impaired diastolic filling and inefficient LV function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syncope : Caused by reduced cardiac output during exertion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sudden Death : The most common cause of sudden death in HOCM is ventricular fibrillation.High risk during intense physical activity, especially in young athletes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distinct Pulse Characteristics : Jerky arterial pulse, distinguishing it from aortic stenosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Angina : Due to increased myocardial oxygen demand during exertion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnoea : Resulting from impaired diastolic filling and inefficient LV function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syncope : Caused by reduced cardiac output during exertion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sudden Death : The most common cause of sudden death in HOCM is ventricular fibrillation.High risk during intense physical activity, especially in young athletes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diastolic heart failure due to a dilated left ventricle (Option A) : HOCM involves hypertrophy, not dilation, of the left ventricle."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mitral valve prolapse (Option B) : Not characteristic of HOCM ; instead, the systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve may lead to mitral regurgitation ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aortic stenosis (Option D) : While both conditions present a murmur, the arterial pulse in HOCM is typically jerky , unlike the slow-rising pulse in aortic stenosis."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 60-year-old male presents with signs of peripheral oedema and ascites. A chest X-ray reveals the following, and echocardiography confirms a thickened pericardium. Which of the following is the least accurate regarding his likely condition?","o":[{"t":"Conservative management with loop diuretics is curative"},{"t":"JVP shows a prominent “y” descent"},{"t":"Commonly caused by tuberculosis in endemic regions"},{"t":"CT scan can assist in visualizing pericardial calcification"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s presentation with peripheral oedema, ascites, elevated JVP, pericardial calcification on X-ray, and a thickened pericardium on echocardiography is suggestive of constrictive pericarditis . This condition is associated with diastolic dysfunction and signs of right-sided heart failure due to a rigid pericardium restricting heart filling."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diuretics may help reduce symptoms but do not cure constrictive pericarditis; the definitive treatment is pericardiectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Purpose/Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chest X-ray Detects pericardial calcification, a key diagnostic clue."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Echocardiography Visualises thickened pericardium and assesses cardiac"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"function."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CT scanning Helps in visualising pericardial calcification."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complex echo-Doppler and cardiac Used to differentiate constrictive pericarditis from catheterisation restrictive cardiomyopathy when diagnosis is uncertain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Manageme Treatment Purpose/Details nt Type"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medical Diuretics (e.g., loop Helps manage diastolic heart failure Manageme diuretics, spironolactone) by reducing fluid overload. nt"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Surgical Pericardiectomy Definitive treatment through surgical Manageme removal of the diseased nt pericardium. Significant"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"improvement is possible, but the procedure has high complication risk and suboptimal outcomes in up to 50% of patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consider constrictive pericarditis in any patient with unexplained right heart failure and a small heart ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"JVP shows a prominent “y” descent (Option B): Reflects rapid ventricular filling with abrupt limitation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Commonly caused by tuberculosis in endemic regions (Option C): Tuberculosis is a primary cause of constrictive pericarditis in areas where it is prevalent."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CT scan can assist in visualizing pericardial calcification (Option D): CT Imaging can help detect pericardial calcifications and thickness."}],"qi":[4282]},{"n":18,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents with fatigue, abdominal swelling, and peripheral oedema. On examination, he has elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP), hepatomegaly, and a loud third heart sound. There is also a rise in JVP during inspiration (see image below). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction"},{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient has classic features like elevated JVP with a rapid y descent, pericardial knock (loud 3rd heart sound), Kussmaul’s sign (Paradoxical rise in JVP during inspiration), hepatomegaly , and peripheral oedema , all suggestive of constriction around the heart."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Constrictive Pericarditis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive thickening, fibrosis, and calcification of the pericardium restrict heart filling."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcification can extend into the myocardium, impairing myocardial contraction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Restricted ventricular filling leads to elevated atrial pressures, causing: Atrial hypertrophy Atrial dilation Atrial fibrillation (common)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial hypertrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial dilation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (common)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily manifests as systemic venous congestion (lung congestion is rare)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial hypertrophy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial dilation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial fibrillation (common)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Examination Findings:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Finding Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Elevated JVP with rapid y descent Reflects rapid early diastolic filling of the right ventricle"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"due to pressure gradient."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fatigue Caused by the heart’s inability to meet circulatory"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"demands."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Rapid, low-volume pulse Restricted filling limits stroke volume, weakening the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pulse."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pericardial knock A loud early S3 from the thickened pericardium during"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diastolic filling."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Kussmaul’s sign Paradoxical rise in JVP during inspiration."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatomegaly Liver enlargement due to i ncreased venous pressure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ascites Abdominal fluid accumulation due to systemic venous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"congestion."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Peripheral oedema Swelling in the extremities from fluid retention."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pulsus paradoxus A fall in systolic BP (10mm Hg) during inspiration,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"potentially impalpable pulse."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atrial fibrillation Common due to atrial hypertrophy and dilation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (Option A) : While fatigue and oedema are present, key signs like Kussmaul’s sign and pericardial knock point towards constrictive pericarditis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dilated cardiomyopathy (Option C) : This typically presents with heart failure symptoms, but the pericardial knock and Kussmaul’s sign are not seen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy (Option D) : Similar to constrictive pericarditis, but without Kussmaul’s sign and pericardial knock , which are characteristic of pericardial disease."}],"qi":[4286]},{"n":19,"q":"A 25 y/o male is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed car accident. He has a deep laceration on his thigh and is actively bleeding. His vitals are heart rate of 120 bpm, blood pressure of 80/50 mmHg, and he is pale with cold, clammy skin. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management step?","o":[{"t":"Administer intravenous antibiotics"},{"t":"Perform an urgent CT scan"},{"t":"Start intravenous fluid resuscitation"},{"t":"Apply a tourniquet to the thigh"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient is in hypovolemic shock due to significant blood loss, which is indicated by the weak vital signs. Immediate fluid resuscitation is critical to restore circulating blood volume while managing the active bleeding."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypovolemic and Obstructive Shock:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aspect Hypovolemic Shock Obstructive Shock"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definitio Critical blood loss leads to Obstruction of blood flow, impairing n inadequate organ perfusion. the heart's ability to pump blood."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes Traumatic injuries (deep lacerations, Injuries like tension pneumothorax,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"internal bleeding) from crashes. and cardiac tamponade in crashes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sympto Dizziness, rapid heartbeat, cold Rapid heartbeat, difficulty breathing, ms skin, pale lips, nausea, confusion. cold skin, anxiety."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathoph Initial compensation fails, leading to Physical blockage reduces oxygen ysiology low blood pressure, and multi-organ delivery to organs."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"failure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatme Volume resuscitation (IV fluids, Relieve obstruction (needle nt blood transfusions), control bleeding. decompression, pericardiocentesis),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and fluid resuscitation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer intravenous antibiotics (Option A) : While necessary in trauma cases to prevent infection, it is not the priority in acute shock management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Perform an urgent CT scan (Option B) : Imaging is important for diagnosis but should be deferred until the patient is stabilised."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Apply a tourniquet to the thigh (Option D) : This can be used if bleeding is not controlled by direct pressure, but fluid resuscitation is the immediate priority."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 60 y/o woman presents to the ED with severe shortness of breath and chest tightness. O/E, she is hypotensive with a blood pressure of 85/60 mmHg, has distended neck veins with a jugular venous pressure (JVP) of 12 cm HnO, and her heart sounds are muffled. An echocardiogram shows the following: What is the most appropriate immediate and definitive treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous saline infusion"},{"t":"Pericardiocentesis"},{"t":"Diuretics"},{"t":"Surgical drainage (pericardiotomy)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient's presentation—severe hypotension, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds, and echocardiographic evidence of right ventricular collapse—suggests cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Echocardiography : Primary diagnostic tool. Pericardiocentesis: The diastolic collapse of the right ventricle. Immediate procedure to drain fluid and relieve pressure Assesses pericardial effusion (size, location). on the heart. (Option B) Normal E/A Ratio: In healthy individuals, the E/A ratio is Often guided by echocardiography. typically greater than 1, indicating effective ventricular Intravenous Saline : Given temporarily while getting filling. ready for the procedure. (Option A ruled out) Diastolic Dysfunction: In cardiac tamponade, the E/A Catheter Placement : A small catheter may be left in ratio can decrease to less than 1, signifying impaired place post-pericardiocentesis for ongoing drainage if diastolic filling and relaxation. fluid reaccumulates. Shows respiratory variations in transvalvular flow Surgical Drainage (Subxiphoid Pericardiotomy): velocities. Indicated for recurrent tamponade, loculated effusions, ECG : or ineffective pericardiocentesis. (Option D ruled out) Shows low QRS voltage due to fluid interference. Pericardial Fluid Analysis : Important for identifying the May present electrical alternans, indicating tamponade. underlying cause of the effusion (e.g., cytology, cultures, Right Heart Catheterization : PCR for infections). Provides haemodynamic data. Addressing Underlying Causes : Essential for preventing Reveals elevated and equalized pressures in all cardiac recurrence (e.g., antibiotics for infections, chemotherapy chambers during diastole . for malignancies)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"E/A ratio: Relative velocities of blood flow during early (E) and late (A) diastolic filling of the left ventricle"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diuretics (Option C): These are not useful in cardiac tamponade, as reducing fluid volume could worsen hypotension."}],"qi":[4292]},{"n":21,"q":"Which of the following is a classic finding in cardiac tamponade?","o":[{"t":"Pulsus paradoxus"},{"t":"S3 heart sound"},{"t":"Pulsus alternans"},{"t":"High-pitched systolic murmur"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulsus paradoxus is a hallmark finding in cardiac tamponade , characterized by a significant drop in systolic blood pressure during inspiration (greater than 10 mm Hg). It occurs due to the impaired filling of the heart chambers caused by pericardial fluid compression, limiting cardiac output during inspiration. This phenomenon is commonly observed in cardiac tamponade and serves as a key diagnostic feature in such cases."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiac Tamponade"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition A condition where the cardiac chambers are"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"compressed by a tense pericardial effusion, limiting cardiac inflow. Fluid accumulation of as little as 200 ml to upwards of 2L may lead to cardiac tamponade development."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Beck's triad"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Jugular Venous Distension : Due to increased central venous pressure. Prominent x descent and an absent y descent. Hypotension : Resulting from impaired cardiac output. Muffled Heart Sounds : Caused by fluid accumulation in the pericardial space, dampening heart sounds."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other features Pulsus Paradoxus : A drop in systolic BP during"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inspiration, greater than 10 mm Hg. Causes of Pulsus paradoxus: Lung Conditions: Acute asthma, COPD, tension pneumothorax, pleural effusion, pulmonary embolism. Heart Conditions: Cardiac tamponade, constrictive pericarditis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Low-Pressure Cardiac Tamponade"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition This occurs when lower pericardial pressure leads to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tamponade, often in cases of intravascular fluid depletion (e.g., hypovolemia)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Features Jugular Venous Distension: Often absent"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pulsus Paradoxus: Rare Requires a high degree of suspicion due to the absence of typical signs like jugular venous distension and pulsus paradoxus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"S3 heart sound (Option B): An S3 heart sound, also known as a ventricular gallop, is typically associated with heart failure or conditions causing rapid filling of the ventricles, such as dilated cardiomyopathy. It is not a classic finding in cardiac tamponade, which is more characterized by restricted ventricular filling and muffled heart sounds rather than additional heart sounds like S3."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulsus alternans (Option C): Pulsus alternans refers to alternating strong and weak pulse beats, typically seen in left ventricular dysfunction, not cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-pitched systolic murmur (Option D): A high-pitched systolic murmur is usually associated with valvular heart diseases, such as aortic stenosis or mitral regurgitation, not cardiac tamponade"}]},{"n":22,"q":"A 65-year-old man presents to the emergency department with complaints of shortness of breath, fatigue, and mild chest discomfort. On examination, his blood pressure is low, and his heart sounds are faint on auscultation. The physician orders an echocardiogram, which shows the following Based on these findings, which of the following ECG patterns would you most likely expect to see?","o":[{"t":"High-voltage QRS complexes"},{"t":"ST-segment elevation in all leads"},{"t":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"t":"Electrical alternans"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient’s symptoms, vitals and echocardiographic findings which reveal a large fluid collection around the heart , with evidence of impaired filling of the right-sided chambers are suggestive of cardiac tamponade, likely due to a large pericardial effusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrical alternans is a classic ECG finding in pericardial effusion , particularly when it leads to cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It manifests as beat-to-beat variations in the amplitude of the QRS complexes, reflecting the heart's movement within the fluid-filled pericardial space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This finding is indicative of significant fluid accumulation and increased intrapericardial pressure, which impedes the heart's ability to fill properly during diastole."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG showing Electrical alternans"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Red arrows showing variations in the amplitude of QRS complexes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"alternans with variations in the amplitude of QRS complexes\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"8457\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"514\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"width=\"514\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray showing waterbottle configuration in Pericardial effusion"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-voltage QRS complexes (Option A): High-voltage QRS complexes suggest an increased electrical activity due to conditions like left ventricular hypertrophy or other myocardial adaptations. However, in pericardial effusion, there are low-voltage QRS complexes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ST-segment elevation in all leads (Option B): This finding is more characteristic of acute pericarditis, where widespread ST-segment elevations occur due to inflammation of the pericardial sac. In pericardial effusion or tamponade, ST-segment changes are not the defining features."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval (Option C): A prolonged PR interval typically indicates delayed conduction through the atrioventricular node. Although PR segment depression can occur in acute pericarditis, a prolonged PR interval is not a hallmark of pericardial effusion or tamponade."}],"qi":[4298]},{"n":23,"q":"A 60 y/o male with acute pericarditis who has not responded to NSAIDs and colchicine is now experiencing frequent recurrences. He does not tolerate glucocorticoids well. What additional treatment can be considered?","o":[{"t":"Increase the dose of NSAIDs"},{"t":"Start azathioprine"},{"t":"Begin a higher dose of colchicine"},{"t":"Stop all medications and monitor"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In cases where NSAIDs, colchicine, and glucocorticoids have failed, immunosuppressive therapy such as azathioprine can be considered. Azathioprine is used for patients with recurrent, refractory pericarditis who have multiple episodes lasting for extended periods."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Echocardiography : Conservative Management: The primary tool for diagnosing Rest and anti-inflammatory drugs for idiopathic pericardial fluid. pericarditis. Significant pericardial effusion can be Aspirin/NSAIDs : identified if fluid exceeds 1 cm. Doses: Aspirin (2-4 g/day) or ibuprofen/indomethacin CT & MRI : with gastric protection. Detect loculated effusions, masses, or Continue for 1-2 weeks, and taper over time. inflammation (MRI) Colchicine : Used to confirm pericardial effusion or Dose: 0.5 mg daily (<70 kg) or twice daily (>70 kg) for 3 thickening. months. MRI also assesses fibrosis and Reduces recurrence risk but may cause diarrhoea. constrictive pericarditis. Glucocorticoids :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Only for NSAID/colchicine-resistant cases, short-term use (2-4 days), then tapered. Prolonged use raises the recurrence risk. Immunosuppressants : Azathioprine or anakinra in cases of frequent recurrence is unresponsive to other treatments. Avoid Anticoagulants : Risk of bleeding into the pericardial cavity, leading to tamponade. Surgical: Pericardial stripping for rare, severe cases with recurrent effusion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increase the dose of NSAIDs (Option A): NSAIDs are effective for inflammation control in the short term, but increasing the dosage does not provide additional benefit once recurrences occur."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Begin a higher dose of colchicine (Option C): Increasing the dose beyond the recommended levels is not advisable, as colchicine has a narrow therapeutic window . Higher doses increase the risk of gastrointestinal side effects, such as diarrhoea, and can be toxic, especially in patients with liver or kidney problems."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stop all medications and monitor (Option D): Stopping all medications without an alternative treatment plan is not appropriate in recurrent pericarditis cases. It can lead to persistent discomfort and potential complications."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"4353":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4357":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4247":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4253":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4259":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4265":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4271":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4275":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4282":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4286":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4292":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4298":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4305":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4312":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4318":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4325":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4328":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4331":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4340":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4344":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4347":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","4350":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});